<!DOCTYPE html>
<html xmlns:o='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office' xmlns:w='urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word' xmlns='http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40'>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <title>SOC Analyst Lab: Questions, Hints and Final Answers</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: Calibri, 'Segoe UI', Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 11pt; line-height: 1.5; color: #1e293b; margin: 35px; }
    h1 { color: #0f172a; font-size: 22pt; border-bottom: 2px solid #0f172a; padding-bottom: 6px; margin-bottom: 4px; }
    .subtitle { color: #64748b; font-size: 12pt; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 25px; }
    h2 { color: #047857; font-size: 15pt; border-bottom: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding-bottom: 4px; margin-top: 30px; }
    .q-card { border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px; margin-bottom: 20px; background: #ffffff; }
    .q-title { font-weight: bold; color: #0f172a; font-size: 11.5pt; margin-bottom: 10px; }
    .hint-box { background: #fffbeb; border-left: 4px solid #f59e0b; padding: 8px 12px; margin-bottom: 10px; font-size: 10pt; color: #92400e; }
    .ans-box { background: #f0fdf4; border-left: 4px solid #16a34a; padding: 8px 12px; font-size: 10.5pt; color: #166534; }
    code { font-family: Consolas, 'Courier New', monospace; background: #f1f5f9; padding: 2px 5px; border-radius: 3px; font-size: 10pt; color: #b91c1c; font-weight: bold; }
    table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin-top: 15px; margin-bottom: 20px; }
    th { background: #0f172a; color: white; padding: 8px 10px; text-align: left; font-size: 10pt; }
    td { border: 1px solid #cbd5e1; padding: 8px 10px; font-size: 10pt; }
    .walkthrough-step { background: #f8fafc; border: 1px solid #e2e8f0; border-radius: 6px; padding: 12px; margin-bottom: 12px; }
    .walkthrough-step strong { color: #0369a1; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

  <h1>🛡️ SOC Analyst Lab: Web Attack Forensics & IoC Hunting</h1>
  <p class="subtitle"><strong>Official Lab Guide, Question Bank & Master Answer Key (8 Questions)</strong></p>

  <p><em>This document contains the step-by-step walkthrough to perform the three attacks (SQL Injection Login Bypass, Cross-Site Scripting, and Login Brute Force), followed by the 8 official investigation questions with hints and final answers for your assessment platform.</em></p>

  <hr>

  <h2>Part 1: How to Perform the Lab</h2>
  
  <div class="walkthrough-step">
    <strong>Attack 1: SQL Injection (Login Bypass & Forensic IoC Analysis)</strong><br>
    <strong>Step A:</strong> In the top navigation, ensure you are in the <strong>🌐 Web Application (portal.aegis-corp.com)</strong> tab.<br>
    <strong>Step B:</strong> Click <strong>"Sign In"</strong> in the top navigation bar to open the corporate Single Sign-On (SSO) login form (<code>/auth/login</code>).<br>
    <strong>Step C:</strong> Inject any SQL injection authentication bypass payload into either Username or Password:<br>
    <div style="background: #f1f5f9; padding: 6px 10px; border-radius: 4px; margin: 6px 0; font-family: Consolas, monospace; font-size: 9.5pt;">
      <code>admin' --</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <code>admin' #</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <code>admin'/*</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <code>admin' or '1'='1</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <code>admin' or '1'='1'--</code><br>
      <code>admin' or 1=1</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <code>admin' or 1=1--</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <code>admin') or ('1'='1</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <code>admin') or '1'='1--</code><br>
      <code>1' or '1' = '1</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <code>or 1=1</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <code>or 1=1--</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <code>or 1=1#</code> &nbsp;|&nbsp; <code>or 1=1/*</code>
    </div>
    <strong>Step D:</strong> Click <strong>Sign In</strong>. The backend query evaluates to <code>TRUE</code>, authenticating you as Administrator and returning an <strong>HTTP 302</strong> redirect.<br>
    <strong>Step E:</strong> Retrieve Flag #1 from the green <strong>Administrative Secret Vault</strong> card on the <strong>Admin Home Page</strong>:<br>
    <code style="font-size: 11pt; color: #047857;">BLITZ{5ql1_byp455_4dm1n_h0m3_7d91a}</code><br><br>
    <strong>Step F (Find IoC in Web Logs):</strong> Switch to the <strong>🛡️ SOC SIEM Log Monitor</strong> tab. Locate the <code>POST /auth/login</code> entry with status <code>302</code> and inspect the forensic IoCs:<br>
    &bull; <strong>Request URI:</strong> <code>/auth/login</code><br>
    &bull; <strong>HTTP Method:</strong> <code>POST</code><br>
    &bull; <strong>Response Status Code:</strong> <code>302 Found</code> (Authentication Redirect)<br>
    &bull; <strong>Payload Location:</strong> <code>Request Body</code> (e.g., <code>username=admin' --&password=...</code>)<br>
    &bull; <strong>SIEM Detection Rule:</strong> <code>RULE-SEC-002</code> (SQL Injection - Authentication Bypass / Tautology Probe)<br>
    &bull; <strong>Client IP:</strong> <code>203.0.113.195</code><br>
    <strong>Step G:</strong> Paste these values into <strong>Question 1, Question 2, and Question 3</strong> in the questionnaire below.
  </div>

  <div class="walkthrough-step">
    <strong>Attack 2: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS in Search Bar)</strong><br>
    1. In the <strong>🌐 Web Application</strong> tab, navigate to the <strong>Home & Search</strong> view.<br>
    2. In the <strong>Enterprise Resource & Asset Search</strong> bar, enter an executable JavaScript payload:
       <br><code>&lt;script&gt;alert('XSS')&lt;/script&gt;</code><br>
    3. Press <strong>Enter</strong> or click <strong>Search</strong>.<br>
    4. The unsanitized input is reflected into the DOM, executing within the browser context and triggering a security context modal displaying the exposed session cookie and the challenge flag:
       <br><code>BLITZ{x55_r3fl3ct3d_s34rch_9b42e}</code><br>
    5. Switch to the <strong>🛡️ SOC SIEM Log Monitor</strong> tab, click the <strong>XSS</strong> quick filter, and inspect the logged <code>GET /api/search?q=...</code> access event to examine the raw URL percent encoding (<code>%3C</code> and <code>%3E</code>).
  </div>

  <div class="walkthrough-step">
    <strong>Attack 3: Authentication Brute Force Analysis</strong><br>
    1. Open the <strong>🛡️ SOC SIEM Log Monitor</strong> tab.<br>
    2. Click the <strong>"Brute Force"</strong> filter pill or search for <code>status:401</code>.<br>
    3. In the status code timeline histogram and log table, observe a rapid spike of consecutive <strong>HTTP 401 Unauthorized</strong> responses targeting <code>POST /auth/login</code> from external source IP <strong><code>203.0.113.89</code></strong>.<br>
    4. Inspect the log entries to identify the targeted corporate username: <strong><code>sarah_admin</code></strong>.<br>
    5. Locate the final log entry from that same IP address (<code>203.0.113.89</code>) which abruptly transitioned from status <strong>401</strong> to <strong>HTTP 302 Found</strong> (Authentication Success).<br>
    6. Click <strong>"Inspect Log"</strong> to review the request body and find the compromised password:
       <br><code>Summer2026!</code>
  </div>

  <hr>

  <h2>Part 2: Official Questions, Hints & Final Answers</h2>

  <!-- Question 1 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 1: SQL Injection Login Bypass Flag</div>
    <p>Perform a SQL injection authentication bypass on the corporate login page (<code>/auth/login</code>) using a payload such as <code>' OR '1'='1</code> or <code>admin' --</code> to gain unauthorized access to the Admin Home Page. What is the administrative secret flag displayed inside the vault?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> Click "Sign In", input <code>admin' --</code> or <code>' OR '1'='1</code> into the login credentials, and submit. The application redirects to the Admin Home Page where the green Administrative Secret Vault card presents the flag.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>BLITZ{5ql1_byp455_4dm1n_h0m3_7d91a}</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 2 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 2: HTTP Login Redirection Status Code</div>
    <p>In standard web application protocols, what 3xx HTTP redirection status code is returned by the server upon successful authentication to redirect the client's browser to the Admin Home Page?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> Check the SOC SIEM access logs for the successful <code>/auth/login</code> request. Web servers issue this standard redirection code (Found / Moved Temporarily) before the browser navigates to the landing dashboard.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>302</code> <em>(also accept: <code>302 Found</code>, <code>302 Redirect</code>)</em>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 3 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 3: HTTP Protocol & SQLi Payload Transmission</div>
    <p>When submitting credentials on the login page via a <code>POST /auth/login</code> request, in which specific component of the HTTP packet is the SQL injection payload transmitted (unlike <code>GET</code> requests which transmit parameters in the URI query string)?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> In HTTP POST requests, form variables and credentials are sent inside the message payload section beneath the HTTP request headers.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>Request Body</code> <em>(also accept: <code>Body</code>, <code>HTTP Body</code>, <code>POST Body</code>)</em>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 4 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 4: Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) Execution Flag</div>
    <p>Enter an executable JavaScript payload (<code>&lt;script&gt;alert('XSS')&lt;/script&gt;</code>) into the enterprise resource search bar. What challenge flag is displayed when the unsanitized input is reflected and executed by the browser?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> In the Web Application tab, enter <code>&lt;script&gt;alert('XSS')&lt;/script&gt;</code> into the search bar and press Enter. A browser execution sandbox modal will trigger displaying the flag.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>BLITZ{x55_r3fl3ct3d_s34rch_9b42e}</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 5 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 5: Web Server Access Log URL Encoding</div>
    <p>When inspecting raw web server access logs in the SOC SIEM console for the reflected XSS attack on <code>/api/search?q=</code>, web browsers URL-encode special characters. What is the two-character hexadecimal percent-encoding representation for the opening angle bracket character (<code>&lt;</code>)?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> Open the SIEM console, find the <code>GET /api/search</code> XSS log, and inspect the raw URI string. The ASCII code for <code>&lt;</code> is 60, which corresponds to this hexadecimal value prefixed by <code>%</code>.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>%3C</code> <em>(also accept: <code>%3c</code>)</em>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 6 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 6: Brute Force HTTP Status Code Spike</div>
    <p>During an automated credential stuffing or dictionary brute force attack against a web login portal, which HTTP client error response code spikes rapidly in the SIEM telemetry, signifying failed authentication attempts?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> In HTTP RFC specifications, this 4xx status code officially designates "Unauthorized" when user credentials are missing or invalid.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>401</code> <em>(also accept: <code>401 Unauthorized</code>)</em>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 7 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 7: Threat Actor IP Identification</div>
    <p>Analyze the historical authentication logs in the SOC SIEM console for brute force activity targeting the user account <code>sarah_admin</code>. What external source IP address conducted this high-frequency password guessing attack?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> In the SIEM console, search for <code>status:401</code> or filter by the "Brute Force" category. Look at the Client IP column of the repeated failed login attempts against <code>/auth/login</code>.
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>203.0.113.89</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Question 8 -->
  <div class="q-card">
    <div class="q-title">Question 8: Compromised Credential Discovery</div>
    <p>Inspect the request body of the successful authentication event (<code>HTTP 302</code>) originating from attacker IP <code>203.0.113.89</code> that immediately followed the repeated 401 failures. What cleartext password did the threat actor successfully guess to compromise the account?</p>
    <div class="hint-box">
      <strong>💡 Hint:</strong> In the SIEM console, locate the single <code>302</code> log entry from IP <code>203.0.113.89</code> on URI <code>/auth/login</code>, click "Inspect Log Details", and examine the <code>body</code> field (<code>username=sarah_admin&password=...</code>).
    </div>
    <div class="ans-box">
      <strong>🎯 Final Answer:</strong> <code>Summer2026!</code>
    </div>
  </div>

  <hr>

  <h2>Part 3: Master Flags & Forensic IoC Summary</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Attack Vector</th>
        <th>Target Endpoint</th>
        <th>Forensic Indicator of Compromise (IoC)</th>
        <th>Challenge Flag / Key Detail</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>SQL Injection (Login Bypass)</strong></td>
        <td><code>POST /auth/login</code></td>
        <td>Tautology <code>' OR '1'='1</code> or comment <code>--</code> in body; returns HTTP 302</td>
        <td><code>BLITZ{5ql1_byp455_4dm1n_h0m3_7d91a}</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Cross-Site Scripting (Reflected)</strong></td>
        <td><code>GET /api/search?q=</code></td>
        <td>Unsanitized script tags encoded as <code>%3Cscript%3E</code> in access URI</td>
        <td><code>BLITZ{x55_r3fl3ct3d_s34rch_9b42e}</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Brute Force (Login)</strong></td>
        <td><code>POST /auth/login</code></td>
        <td>Surge of HTTP 401s followed by anomalous HTTP 302 from single IP</td>
        <td>Attacker IP: <code>203.0.113.89</code><br>Password: <code>Summer2026!</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p style="text-align: center; color: #64748b; font-size: 9pt; margin-top: 30px;">
